{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794825210,"nonce":"ahk4FbWRRX7CLixZCKKcFKtTviQ=","message":"Naming, decided 2026-11-16: hxn-gpu's package is hxn.gpu, not gpu, so it cannot collide with another library's top-level package. The entry point is GpuDevice.open(options), which returns a Future\u003cGpuDevice\u003e; backends() and featuresOf() are static on GpuDevice too. There is no separate Gpu class.\n\nOpen: the released libraries use top-level packages (gpu.metal, gpu.vulkan, gpu.d3d12, gpu.gles, gpu.webgpu, gpu.webgl2, window, media). Moving them under hxn.* is one hxn::package! line each, but it breaks their users, so each would need a 0.2.0. hxn-gpu's users are not affected, because the driver packages appear only inside hxn-gpu's backends.\n\nState: all six backends are written. On this Mac (Apple M1 Pro), the check passes on Metal, Vulkan (MoltenVK) and GLES (ANGLE from Electron 43.8.3), under Ash (hybrid, JIT, interpreter) and hxcpp. It also passes on WebGPU and WebGL2 in headless Chrome, under hxcpp, threaded hxcpp and threaded Ash, including one web program with both drivers. Direct3D 12 type-checks against the driver's Windows library and has not run. Driver gaps found: hxn-gpu-webgpu has no per-instance vertex step, no indexed draws and one vertex buffer. hxn-gpu-metal, -vulkan, -gles and -webgl2 have no buffer copies. hxn-gpu-d3d12 copies only whole buffers and has no root offsets. GLES and WebGL2 read back only rgba8, and their rendered images are bottom-up in memory, since hxnsl's GLSL does not flip y.","files":null}]}